{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_88","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-19_13-32-32-dragonboat_raft-real-run/submissions && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json,glob,os\nfs=sorted(glob.glob('*/inputs/update.json'), key=os.path.getmtime)\nfor f in fs[::-1]:\n    try: d=json.load(open(f))\n    except Exception: continue\n    if isinstance(d,dict) and d.get('experiment') and d.get('interpretations') and d.get('sources'):\n        print(f); print(json.dumps(d, indent=1)[:6000]); break\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"545f390aeead46dc8d1f3abd858fd5e3/inputs/update.json\n{\n \"rationale\": \"Seventy-eighth pass: trace what a follower's applied frontier does across a leader-supplied snapshot install, the one place where the node deliberately jumps its pushed index instead of advancing it by one. The trace covers the raft-side adoption gate, the in-memory log replacement, the update commit that clears the attached snapshot, the engine's two-pass ordering around the raft state write, node.pushSnapshot, node.entriesToApply and the state machine's snapshot-task boundary. The result is a negative: entries at or below the installed snapshot are dropped rather than replayed, later committed entries can only be queued behind the recovery task, a redelivered snapshot is refused by the raft layer before it can reach pushSnapshot, and the recovering flag keeps the apply loop away from that cluster until recovery is reported complete. The one asymmetry worth recording is that pushSnapshot advances pushedIndex and the node's snapshot index at queue time, before the asynchronous recovery runs, so the frontier is a promise about queued work rather than a statement about completed recovery; the source shows the queue order and the recovering gate are what keep those two consistent. A focused object-level probe is submitted with this pass so the frontier arithmetic and the task boundary can be confirmed by a retained receipt rather than source reading alone; its interpretation follows the receipt.\",\n \"sources\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"raft_restore_snapshot_gate\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/raft.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 447,\n   \"end_line\": 478,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"logentry_restore_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/logentry.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 395,\n   \"end_line\": 399,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"inmemory_restore_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/inmemory.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 232,\n   \"end_line\": 243,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"inmemory_clear_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/inmemory.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 166,\n   \"end_line\": 172,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"peer_update_snapshot_field\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/peer.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 323,\n   \"end_line\": 347,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"peer_update_commit_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/raft/peer.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 409,\n   \"end_line\": 425,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"node_process_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"node.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 1065,\n   \"end_line\": 1079,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"node_push_snapshot\",\n   \"file\": \"node.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 605,\n   \"end_line\": 621,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"node_entries_to_apply\",\n   \"file\": \"node.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 549,\n   \"end_line\": 571,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"rsm_handle_snapshot_boundary\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/rsm/statemachine.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 547,\n   \"end_line\": 593,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"node_process_recover_status\",\n   \"file\": \"node.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 1428,\n   \"end_line\": 1447,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"rsm_recover_out_of_date_guard\",\n   \"file\": \"internal/rsm/statemachine.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 324,\n   \"end_line\": 350,\n   \"kind\": \"code_observation\"\n  }\n ],\n \"investigations\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"@snapshot_install_frontier\",\n   \"question\": \"When a follower installs a leader-supplied snapshot, does the node keep one consistent applied frontier, so that committed entries at or below the snapshot are not applied on top of the recovered state, later committed entries are offered only after the snapshot recovery, and a snapshot redelivered after the frontier has moved cannot reach the state machine a second time?\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"raft_restore_snapshot_gate\",\n    \"logentry_restore_snapshot\",\n    \"inmemory_restore_snapshot\",\n    \"inmemory_clear_snapshot\",\n    \"peer_update_snapshot_field\",\n    \"peer_update_commit_snapshot\",\n    \"node_process_snapshot\",\n    \"node_push_snapshot\",\n    \"node_entries_to_apply\",\n    \"rsm_handle_snapshot_boundary\",\n    \"node_process_recover_status\",\n    \"rsm_recover_out_of_date_guard\",\n    \"engine_process_steps_order\",\n    \"engine_apply_snapshot_and_update\",\n    \"node_push_entries\"\n   ],\n   \"hypothesis\": \"The raft layer adopts a snapshot only above its committed index, so the snapshot index always exceeds every entry the node has already pushed. node.pushSnapshot then queues a Recover task ahead of the same update's committed entries and moves pushedIndex to the snapshot index, entriesToApply drops everything at or below that frontier, the state machine returns the recovery task before any task queued behind it, and the recovering flag keeps the apply loop away from the cluster until recovery is reported complete. A redelivered snapshot never reaches pushSnapshot again because raft.restore refuses an index at or below committed and because the attached snapshot is cleared when the update commits.\",\n   \"discriminator\": \"Whether any permitted event can deliver a snapshot index at or below the node's current pushed index, or let committed-entry tasks overtake a pending recovery task; the submitted probe observes the frontier values, the filtering of overlapping entries and which task the state machine reports first, while the asynchronous recovery completion gate stays supported by source rather than by that probe.\",\n   \"disposition\": \"active\"\n  }\n ],\n \"knowledge\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"@ki_snapshot_adoption_committed_gate\",\n   \"kind\": \"behavior\",\n   \"statement\": \"raft.restore refuses a snapshot whose index is not above the raft log's committed index and returns false without attaching any snapshot to the update. When the log already holds an entry at the snapshot index with the snapshot term, it only moves the commit index to that snapshot index and returns false. Otherwise it replaces the in-memory log with the snapshot, sets the log's committed and processed indexes to the snapshot index, clears the in-m\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
